this photograph of Marcy borders came to  symbolize the collective trauma New  Yorkers experienced on September 11th  2001  she was nicknamed the dust lady she died  two weeks ago of stomach cancer at the  age of 42 photographers Stan Honda who  took the picture encountered miss  borders in the building next to the  World Trade Center after it collapsed  this woman walks in and completely  covered and dust you really couldn't see  any features or tell what kind of  clothes she got on anything like that it  was very eerie sight and I had only  enough time to take one picture of her  one frame  Susan hair barely escaped the disaster  she was working in a bank on the 68th  floor of tower two she remembers the  moment the first plane struck I got in  an elevator that wasn't supposed to be  working and took it down to the lobby  there I tried to get out onto the plaza  but they had blocked it off and I found  that late it was because the people were  jumping out of Tower 1 I went to get out  on to Greenwich and a police woman told  me don't look down just run as Susan  hare ran she nearly tripped on body  parts she's still haunted by the memory  this is the first time she's found the  strength to return to this spot it took  tremendous effort since the 9/11 attacks  she has suffered from post-traumatic  stress and asthma she's also a cancer  survivor without public assistance she  would never have been able to pay her  medical bills  but the 2.8 billion dollar fund  supported by the James Zadroga 9/11  health and Compensation Act named after  one of the first responders is set to  run out at the end of the year it's very  important to me and to the fellow  survivors of 9/11 the aid that we get  from the Sagara bill helps us pay our  medical bills and also networks us with  a whole bunch of  there's that a familiar with the nine  eleven illnesses both physical and  emotional and mentally one of the  specialists who's treated many 9/11  survivors is dr. Michael crane he's  concerned about what might happen if the  aid program is terminated the  interruption of that care while no  physician to my knowledge would abandon  a patient would eventually lead in a  district to a disruption of the services  at a very critical moment and it could  be yes it could be a tremendous setback  for many of our patients who are being  treated for these critical diseases  that's a view shared by Margaret chin a  democratic representative of the New  York City Council she fears that  Congress in a cost-cutting measure could  slash funding handing responsibility to  New York  we just want Congress to know this is  not a New York issue this is a national  issue because the people that came to  help to do the rescue effort they came  from almost every single state in the  United States Susan hair will be  dependent on public health assistance  for years to come  if Congress ends the funding she and  thousands of other New Yorkers will be  left in the lurch
 

